# Collections and aggregates: Jo Nesbø's two short story collections
#
#   Sjalusimannen og andre fortellinger (Aschehoug, 2021), 7 stories
#   Rotteøya og andre fortellinger (Aschehoug, 2021), 5 stories
#
# Each story is a work of its own (genre entedit:T02, individual short
# stories), and each of the two collections is a work (entedit:T01, short story
# collections) that has its stories as parts.
#
# Combinations of the two collections are NOT works or expressions of their
# own: Aschehoug's 2022 paperback with all 12 stories (under the title of the
# first collection only, "Sjalusimannen og andre fortellinger") embodies both
# collection expressions and the stories, and Robert Ferguson's English
# translation, "The jealousy man and other stories" (Harvill Secker and Knopf,
# 2021), embodies the 12 English story expressions. The English book has two
# parts, "Jealousy" and "Power", that match the collections; whether they
# should be recorded as English expressions of the collections is left open.
#
# The French translations (Céline Romand-Monnier, Gallimard, Série noire) came
# out as two books, "De la jalousie" (2022) and "Rat Island" (2024). The French
# titles of the stories in De la jalousie are from a review (k-libre.fr), not
# from the book; for Rat Island only the title story's French title is known,
# and the other four are left without a French title, as an exercise.
#
# Novels published together: six novels, each with its first Norwegian edition,
# and four publications that hold two of them:
#   - Flaggermusmannen ; Kakerlakkene, as a book (Bokklubben 2006) and as an
#     audiobook (Lydbokforlaget 2008) that reuses the readings first published
#     in 1999 and 2005
#   - Snømannen ; Panserhjerte, an audiobook (2010) from the readings of 2007
#     and 2009
#   - Blood on snow : Der Auftrag & Das Versteck (Ullstein 2018), the German
#     translations of Blod på snø and Mere blod, under a collective title found
#     only in this edition
# The two novels are only put together in the publication: there is no omnibus
# work or expression, and the manifestation embodies the two novels'
# expressions directly. This differs from the story collections, which are
# works of their own.
#
# Two authors in one book: Stemmer fra Balkan (Aschehoug, 1999) holds two
# non-fiction texts from the war in Kosovo and Serbia in 1999, Jo Nesbø's
# interviews "Stemmer fra Balkan" and Espen Søbye's diary "Atten dager i mai".
# The book has no collective title (title proper is the first text's, and the
# second text and its author follow in the statement of responsibility), so as
# with the novels there is no aggregate work: the manifestation embodies the two
# expressions directly.
#
# Single stories on their own:
#   - the audio drama Sjalusimannen (Aschehoug 2021), four episodes published
#     one by one; a work of its own based on the story (rdawo:P10142), with the
#     episodes as parts
#   - Danish audiobooks of single stories (Modtryk 2024), translated by Allan
#     Hilton Andersen and read by Bent Otto Hansen: London, Tilståelsen and
#     Rotteøen. The publisher numbers them in series named after the
#     collections, so the series statement is the only trace of the collection.
#
# A manifestation embodies the expression of the collection (or omnibus) and
# the expressions of every story it contains. The order of the stories is not
# recorded (it needs RDF-star, entedit:valueOrder), so lists fall back to title
# order.
#
# Sources: Nasjonalbiblioteket (api.nb.no) for the Norwegian editions, BnF for
# the French, library catalogues and the publishers for the English.
# Identifiers are ad hoc (http://example.org/nesbo/...).

@prefix entedit: <http://oslomet.no/abi/vocab#> .
@prefix rdaad: <http://rdaregistry.info/Elements/a/datatype/> .
